Step 1 — Create a temporary directory

Because you shouldn't modify the original data.txt, create a working directory:

mkdir /tmp/test1

Then enter it:

cd /tmp/test1
Step 2 — Copy the original file

Copy the Level 12 file into your working directory:

cp ~/data.txt .

Now check:

ls

You should see:

data.txt
Step 3 — Convert the hexdump back into binary

The original data.txt contains a hexadecimal dump.

First convert it back to its original binary form:

xxd -r data.txt data8.bin

Now check the file:

file data8.bin

You may get:

data8.bin: gzip compressed data

This tells us that the file is GZIP compressed.

Step 4 — Rename the file

Because it is GZIP data, give it a .gz extension:

mv data8.bin data.gz

Check:

ls

You should see:

data.gz
Step 5 — Decompress the GZIP file

Run:

gzip -d data.gz

This extracts the file.

Now check:

ls

You should have another file, such as:

data
Step 6 — Identify the new file

Don't assume what the new file is.

Always use:

file data

For example, it might say:

data: bzip2 compressed data

That means the next layer is BZIP2.

Step 7 — Rename it if necessary

For example:

mv data data.bz2

Then decompress:

bzip2 -d data.bz2

Check the result:

ls

Then identify it again:

file data
Step 8 — Repeat the process

This is the most important part of Level 12.

Every time you get a new file:

① Identify it
file data
② Rename it according to its format

Examples:

mv data data.gz
mv data data.bz2
mv data data.tar
③ Extract it

For GZIP:

gzip -d data.gz

For BZIP2:

bzip2 -d data.bz2

For TAR:

tar -xf data.tar

Then identify the resulting file again:

file data
Step 9 — Why file is so important

You cannot simply keep using gzip -d.

For example:

file data

might tell you:

gzip compressed data

So use:

gzip -d data.gz

Later it might say:

bzip2 compressed data

So now use:

bzip2 -d data.bz2

Later it could say:

POSIX tar archive

So use:

tar -xf data.tar

The file command tells you what extraction method to use next.

Step 10 — Continue until you reach ASCII text

Eventually:

file data

will show something like:

data: ASCII text

That means you've reached the final readable file.

Now run:

cat data

You'll see:

The password is XXXXXXXXXXXXX

That is the password for Bandit Level 13.

bandit13: qQYQiHOBPR8zR61qxYqX45quvihF2uzk

